115752 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186439083 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186115752 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186439084 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186115752 #edb/ref 1069 "q-32242" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115752 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115752 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-x+2y &=6 \\\\ 2x-3y &=13 \\\\ 5x-y &=45\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The system is consistent dependent\n- **II.** The system is inconsistent\n- **III.** The system has a unique solution" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186115752 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "402ab26a-a0df-5fc5-a264-b91a4c6d7d02" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115752 #edb/ref 1074 "To classify the system, we first need to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 13 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 45\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 13 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 45\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 25 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 45\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 25 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 75\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-9){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 25 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -150\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final row corresponds to the equation $0x+0y=-150,$ and this has no solution. Therefore, our system has no solution, and we conclude that the system is inconsistent.\n\nAs a result, only statement II is true." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186115753 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301729 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115753 #edb/ref 1069 "q-32246" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115753 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115753 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is consistent dependent:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+4y &=12 \\\\ x+ky &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186115753 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2a9a3feb-6c16-50e8-885d-ee5cd242ab95" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115753 #edb/ref 1074 "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 & 12 \\\\ 1 & k & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 & 12 \\\\ 1 & k & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & k & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 12\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & k & 4 \\\\ 0 & 4-3k & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+ky &=4 \\\\ (4-3k)y &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe system is consistent dependent if it has infinitely many solutions. For the system to have infinitely many solutions, it must have at least one free variable.\n\nIf $k=\\frac{4}{3},$ then the second equation is $0=0,$ which is true for any value of $y$, and we can remove this equation from the system. As a result, the system will contain only one equation, $x+ky=4.$ \n\nThen, the basic variable will be $x,$ and we will have a free variable $y.$ That means the system will have infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, our system is consistent dependent when $k=\\frac{4}{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186116120 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116121 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116120 #edb/ref 1061 17213 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116120 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6022e9b9-60ab-5ac5-b16a-17583fa649c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116120 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186116493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116121 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116122 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116121 #edb/ref 1061 17214 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116121 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "79bb6bd1-8c36-5f56-9575-8a1b8388eb57" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116121 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115241 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116122 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116209 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116122 #edb/ref 1061 17215 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116122 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2fa6eba1-6298-51b5-bbc9-bd8c46a79468" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116122 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186116494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116123 #edb/ref 1061 17216 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116123 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1d1a97f8-c872-5285-b963-d1938948df37" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116123 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115242 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116209 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116123 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116209 #edb/ref 1061 23072 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116209 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e80cfcd9-1219-52d8-96c7-0ee06cdf52fc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116209 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115299 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116493 #edb/ref 1064 "We can use Gaussian elimination to find the general solutions of systems with any number of equations and variables. For instance, consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y+z &=1, \\\\ y+3z &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system has the augmented matrix\n![](images/8a/8a8452587098eed9b697c3999c356359b949429f29ce16af2c81cf728be9480e.svg)\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\, & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+4z &=1 \\\\ y+3z &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe see that the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable $z.$ From the second equation, we get $y=-3z,$ and from the first equation, we obtain $x=1-4z.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1-4z, \\\\ y &=-3z, \\\\ z &=z.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1-4z \\\\ -3z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+z\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186116493 #edb/ref 1065 3162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116493 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3eff050b-e4ba-52e5-aa10-7bba2feef2fc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116493 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116494 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that a system of linear equations is called **consistent** if it has at least one solution. Otherwise, if there are no solutions, the system is called **inconsistent**. \n\nFor a consistent system, \n\n- the system is called **consistent independent** if the solution is unique, while\n\n- the system is called **consistent dependent** if there are infinitely many solutions.\n\n![](images/f5/f5b808eac6e1d865b13b68ce2a4f7d317560ae3965928e478531fcd2148c2c6b.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116494 #edb/ref 1065 3163 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116494 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e0c85152-daea-5a87-a3ba-81870558c7a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116494 #edb/ref 1067 "Consistent and Inconsistent Systems of Linear Equations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263196 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}5{x}_{3}-4 \\\\ 1-2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186263196 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263196 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "150b8c63-cad2-507c-af99-2caf46c53ce7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263546 #edb/ref 1080 "No solution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263546 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263546 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3d6a91da-fd38-559c-b128-19dbc7c776d0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263588 #edb/ref 1080 "k=-\\frac{2}{7}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263588 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263588 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "427da7e5-15ac-51bd-a759-d90b6c65d454" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263720 #edb/ref 1080 "k=-7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263720 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263720 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "538a47b2-6a0b-5a53-99e0-b4e09df09626" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263881 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}5{x}_{3}+4 \\\\ 2{x}_{3}+1 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186263881 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263881 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "65da6370-1e54-53a6-8151-ac1ec3ef9e6f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264099 #edb/ref 1080 "k=\\frac{7}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264099 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264099 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7f383d71-c4b1-501b-823d-dfdddaac22d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264378 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{3}+1 \\\\ 5{x}_{3}-1 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186264378 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264378 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a2aede61-88b5-5a76-9ec8-106c89064823" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264574 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}4-5{x}_{3} \\\\ 1-2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186264574 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264574 #edb/ref 1083 #uu